course: geography
lesson: 53
title: Rivers shaping the land
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A river is a long conveyor belt for water and rock. It begins at its source, often a spring or a boggy patch in the hills, and ends at its mouth, where it meets the sea, a lake or another river.
explain: High up, the river is fast and steep and cuts down into the land, making a deep V-shaped valley. Where it pours over a band of hard rock lying on soft rock, the soft rock wears back faster and a waterfall forms.
explain: Lower down the river slows and swings in big loops called meanders, like a snake. If it cuts across the neck of a loop, the old loop is left behind as an oxbow lake.
explain: Floods spread mud on flat land beside the river, making a fertile flood plain, and at the mouth the river may drop its mud to build a delta. Over millions of years the Colorado River cut the Grand Canyon about 1.8 km deep.
fact: A river flows from its source in the hills to its mouth at the sea, a lake or another river.
fact: Near the source, a river cuts down into the land, making steep V-shaped valleys.
fact: Waterfalls form where a river flows over hard rock lying on top of softer rock, and as the soft rock wears away the waterfall slowly moves back upstream.
fact: In its lower course, a river winds in big loops called meanders.
fact: An oxbow lake forms when a river cuts through the narrow neck of a meander and leaves the loop behind.
fact: Flood plains are flat lands beside rivers built up from mud left by floods, and they are very fertile.
fact: A delta forms where a river drops its sediment at the mouth faster than the sea can carry it away.
fact: An estuary is a river mouth where fresh water meets the tide, and many great ports grew on them.
fact: The Colorado River carved the Grand Canyon, about 1.8 km deep, over roughly the last 5-6 million years, although some scientists argue parts are older.
fact: River floods are among the deadliest natural disasters, especially on big rivers like the Yellow River in China.
fact: The world's rivers carry billions of tonnes of sediment to the sea every year.
exercise: Where does a river begin? | At its source, often in the hills | At its mouth, where it meets the sea | In the middle of a delta | Inside an oxbow lake on the plain
exercise: What is a river's mouth? | Where it meets the sea, a lake or another river | The steep place high in the hills where it starts | A waterfall halfway down | A deep hole in the riverbed
exercise: What shape of valley does a river cut near its source? | A steep V-shape | A wide U-shape like a glacier's | A perfect circle | A flat square with sharp corners
exercise: Why does a waterfall form? | Hard rock lies on top of softer rock that wears back faster | The river suddenly gets much colder and drops down to the bottom | Fish dig a hole in the riverbed | The Moon pulls the water over the edge
exercise: What are meanders? | Big loops a river winds in, lower down | Steep drops over hard rock near the source | Underground rivers in caves | Sandbanks at the river's source
exercise: How does an oxbow lake form? | The river cuts through the neck of a meander and leaves the loop | A glacier digs a round hole by the sea and fills it with meltwater | Rain fills a crater from a volcano | Beavers dam the river near its source
exercise: Why are flood plains good for farming? | Floods leave fertile mud on them | They never get any water at all | They are covered in sand and stones | They are high up on cold mountains
exercise: When does a delta form? | When a river drops sediment at its mouth faster than the sea removes it | When a river freezes solid in winter and stops dropping any of its mud | When a river runs uphill | When a waterfall wears back to the source
exercise: What is an estuary? | A river mouth where fresh water meets the tide | A lake left behind by a meander when it is cut off | A steep valley near the source | A waterfall over hard rock
exercise: Why did many great ports grow up on estuaries? | Ships could come in from the sea up the river mouth | Estuaries are the highest places in a country | Estuaries never have tides | Estuaries are far from the sea
exercise: Which river carved the Grand Canyon? | The Colorado River | The Nile in Egypt | The River Thames | The Amazon in Brazil
exercise: About how deep is the Grand Canyon? | About 1.8 km | About 18 metres | About 18 km down | About 180 km down
exercise: About how long has the Colorado River been carving the Grand Canyon? | Roughly 5-6 million years | About 500 years, since Columbus | About 50,000 years | About 5 billion years, since the Earth began
exercise: Which Chinese river is known for some of the deadliest floods in history? | The Yellow River | The Danube in Europe | The Mississippi | The Rhine in Germany
exercise: How much sediment do the world's rivers carry to the sea each year? | Billions of tonnes | A few buckets of mud | About a hundred tonnes | None at all, rivers are clean
test: Why does a river cut a V-shaped valley up in the hills but wind in loops near the sea? | It is steep and fast near the source, slower and flatter lower down | It is colder in the hills and warmer by the sea, so the water bends | Fish make the loops near the sea | The wind blows the river into loops
test: "rivers start at the sea and flow up into the mountains" — Jack's little brother. What is right? | Rivers flow downhill from their source to their mouth | Rivers flow uphill in the morning only, then back down at night | Rivers flow both ways at once | Rivers flow only in winter
test: A geologist finds a curved, horseshoe-shaped lake beside a winding river. What is it most likely? | An oxbow lake, a cut-off meander | A crater from a meteorite | A pool dug by a glacier | A spring bubbling up at the river's source
test: Hard rock sits on soft rock where a river drops. What will happen to the waterfall over time? | It will slowly move back upstream | It will slowly climb uphill | It will stay in exactly the same place | It will turn into a delta
test: What would happen to a delta if the sea's waves and currents became much stronger? | The sea might carry the sediment away and the delta could shrink | The delta would grow much faster, as the waves push more mud onto it | The river would flow backwards | The delta would become a mountain
test: mate: "why'd ancient farmers live right next to rivers, they'd just get flooded, thick or what" — what's the best reason? | Floods left fertile mud, so the land grew good crops | River land was always dry and safe | The rivers kept animals away | Floods never happened in ancient times
test: In a cartoon, a river carves a canyon a mile deep in one afternoon. What is wrong? | Canyons like the Grand Canyon take millions of years | Canyons are made by the wind blowing sand, never by rivers | Canyons are dug by people | Canyons form only under the sea
test: Why does a river flowing into a calm sea build a delta while one flowing into a stormy sea may not? | Calm seas cannot carry the sediment away fast enough | Stormy seas have no salt | Calm seas push rivers backwards | Stormy seas freeze the sediment
test: Why are big river floods so deadly? | Many people live and farm on the flat land beside rivers | Rivers flood only in empty deserts, far from any towns | Flood water is boiling hot | Floods happen without any water
test: Which word fits: the Thames meets the tide in a wide mouth where London's port grew. | Estuary | Delta plain | Oxbow lake | Source spring
test: Which order goes from source to mouth? | V-shaped valley, meanders, mouth | Mouth, meanders, source | Delta, waterfall, source | Meanders, source, V-shaped valley
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a waterfall forms in the course of a river. | A stratum of resistant rock overlies a less resistant stratum | The river is subjected to a sudden decrease in temperature | Aquatic creatures excavate the riverbed | The river attains its greatest width
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the formation of an oxbow lake? | A river breaches the narrow neck of a meander and abandons the loop | A glacier excavates a circular basin which subsequently fills with meltwater | A volcanic crater becomes filled with rain | A river is impounded by a dam near its source
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning flood plains. | They are level lands built of deposited silt and are highly fertile | They are steep slopes of bare rock | They are deserts devoid of sediment | They are formed exclusively by wind
exam: Ascertain the conditions under which a delta is formed at the mouth of a river. | Sediment is deposited faster than the sea is able to remove it | The river is entirely frozen throughout the year | The sea deposits sediment into the source of the river from its mouth | The river flows uphill towards its mouth
